<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<speak version="1.1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xml:lang="en-GB">
<!-- The Watch Below, Chapter 66: The Ruin. Point of view: Calder. -->
<p><s>Chapter Sixty-six.</s></p><break time="800ms"/>
<p><s>The Ruin.</s></p><break time="1800ms"/>
<!-- p1 -->
<p>He sent them north on the hundred-and-thirty-ninth day.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p2 -->
<p>He’d thought about it all night on the Scar, with the campfires coming out on the hill behind Harrow’s End one after another like stars, and the carts on the Way. And in the morning he went into the cave where the three of them were lying, and told them.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p3 · Calder -->
<p>“North,” he said. “To the hatching caves. Above <sub alias="Cold-tarn">Coldtarn</sub>. Where we wintered.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p4 -->
<p><sub alias="Karrak">Kharrak</sub> lifted his head off his forelegs.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p5 · Kharrak (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">No.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p6 · Calder -->
<p>“They’ll be on the island tomorrow,” said <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>. “Thousands. With nets and chains and honey-smoke and bound hounds. If they see you over the island, if they see one of you, the whole Ram’s Head comes down on it at once. On you. They want you. They want you more than anything on this side of the river.” He looked at <sub alias="Karrak">Kharrak</sub>. “And I can’t let them take you. I can’t. I’ve seen what they do.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p7 · Kharrak (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">Then we’ll burn them.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p8 · Calder -->
<p>“Thousands, <sub alias="Karrak">Kharrak</sub>.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p9 · Kharrak (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">Then we’ll burn thousands.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p10 · grief: he makes himself say it · Calder -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">“And they’ll bring the smoke. And the nets. And the collars.” He made himself say it. “And one morning I’ll look up and you’ll be standing in a field at the Ram’s Head with a man’s hand on your neck and nothing in your eyes.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p11 -->
<p><sub alias="Karrak">Kharrak</sub> didn’t answer that.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p12 · Aurveth (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">And you?</prosody> said <sub alias="Orveth">Aurveth</sub>. <prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">What will you do?</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p13 · Calder -->
<p>“Go to the island.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p14 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">The cave went very quiet.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p15 · Calder -->
<p>“Alone,” said <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>. “Before they get there. Tonight. I want to see it. The arch. The verse. Whatever’s under the tower. I want to see it before they do.” He looked at <sub alias="Orveth">Aurveth</sub>. “You read the first line, in the freeze. Through me. And you stopped.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p16 · Aurveth (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">Yes.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p17 · Calder -->
<p>“I’ll bring you the rest.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p18 · Aurveth (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">You can’t read it.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p19 · Calder -->
<p>“I’ll bring it anyway.”</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p20 -->
<p><sub alias="Karrak">Kharrak</sub> wouldn’t go.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p21 · tension · Kharrak (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">He lay at the mouth of the cave with his wings half open and the red tide up and said <prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">no</prosody> to everything, over and over, until <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> stopped talking. Then <sub alias="Orveth">Aurveth</sub> got up. She got up in no hurry, and went to the mouth of the cave and stood over <sub alias="Karrak">Kharrak</sub>, with her one gold eye looking down at him and her great bronze head lowered, and said something. <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> didn’t hear it. It wasn’t for him. It went between them, the way it did sometimes, kin to kin, and he only felt the edge of it through the marks, like the edge of a wind.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p22 -->
<p><sub alias="Karrak">Kharrak</sub> folded his wings.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p23 · tender: Lisska crying silently -->
<p><prosody rate="95%"><sub alias="Liska">Lisska</sub> cried. She didn’t make a sound. He felt it, through the green mark, all the way down. She put her head against his chest in the cave and he put his arms round as much of it as he could and they stayed like that until <sub alias="Orveth">Aurveth</sub> said it was time.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p24 · Lisska (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">I’ll feel you,</prosody> said <sub alias="Liska">Lisska</sub>. <prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">Won’t I? Even up there? Even if I can’t hear you?</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p25 · Calder -->
<p>“You’ll feel me.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p26 · Lisska (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">And if something happens?</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p27 · Calder -->
<p>“You’ll feel that too.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p28 · Lisska (bond, to Calder) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%" volume="-2dB">And then can we come?</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p29 -->
<p>He didn’t answer for a moment.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p30 · Calder -->
<p>“Then you can come,” he said.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p31 · grief -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">They went up out of the Scar in the noon light, north, one after another, the bronze and the red and the green, low over the Fellwood toward the <sub alias="Scar-fell">Scarfell</sub>, and he stood on the top of the black rock and watched them go until they were three specks over the white hills, and then two, and then nothing.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p32 · reflective -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">He felt them. All afternoon. Going away. Getting further. The voices first, going thin, the way they had on the drove road to Wendmouth: three leagues, four, and then nothing. And under it, where the voices had been, the other thing. The feeling. Like sheep on a far hill at night: you couldn’t see them, but you knew where each one was. Three of them. Far off. Not happy. There.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p33 -->
<p>He went down to the <sub alias="Eel-weer">Eelweir</sub> at dusk.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p34 -->
<p>The long way. Round through the Fellwood and down Hollow Beck, in the carter’s coat, with Joss’s knife in his belt and the two chains and the ring inside his coat, and a roll of Ada’s paper and a stick of charcoal in his pocket. The paper with the six hands on the back.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p35 -->
<p>He’d thought about the charcoal all morning. He couldn’t read the old script. Nobody he knew could. But every farmer in the Marches knew how to take a rubbing of a boundary stone when there was a dispute: a sheet of paper over the cut letters, and a stick of charcoal, rubbed flat and hard, until the letters came up white out of the black. His father had done it once with the stone at the top of the Fenn field, in a quarrel with old Garrow about a hedge. You didn’t have to read it. You just had to carry it to somebody who could.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p36 -->
<p>The <sub alias="Eel-weer">Eelweir</sub> was full of men.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p37 -->
<p>He lay in the bracken on the slope above it at the edge of the Fellwood and watched them. The landing below, at the mouth of the beck, where Fisk kept his eel-boats and his traps: it was crowded with boats now. Long boats, flat boats, barges, dozens of them, run down off the carts and pulled up on the shingle in rows, with men working at them in the light of fires. More carts coming down the hill behind the village, and more boats coming off them. Men everywhere. In the lanes of Harrow’s End. In the burned mill. In his own top field, he thought, though he couldn’t see it from here. Campfires all the way up the hill.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p38 -->
<p>Fisk’s house was at the end of the landing. Dark. No light in the door.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p39 · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">He’s gone,</prosody> <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> thought. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">They’ve had him out.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p40 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">Then something touched his shoulder, and he nearly cried out.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p41 -->
<p>Fisk was lying in the bracken beside him. On his belly, in the dark, with his stick, and his old face close to <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>’s, and his pipe, unlit, in his teeth.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p42 · humour · Fisk -->
<p>“Took you long enough,” said Fisk.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p43 · Fisk, very low -->
<p><prosody volume="-5dB">“There’s a back way,”</prosody> said Fisk, very low. <prosody volume="-5dB">“Round the top of the island. The north channel, where the beck-water comes across and the reeds are thick. Nobody uses it. Too shallow for anything but an eel-boat, and too twisty for anybody who doesn’t know it.”</prosody> He looked at <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> in the dark. <prosody volume="-5dB">“I know it.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p44 · Calder -->
<p>“They’ll see.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p45 · humour · Fisk -->
<p>“They’ll not see. They’re all looking at the landing. And they don’t know the river.” Fisk spat. “<sub alias="Salt-reach">Saltreach</sub> soup-sellers. They’ve been putting boats in the water since noon and they’ve lost two to the current already. I watched. Laughed till I hurt.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p46 -->
<p>He had an eel-boat in the reeds at the mouth of the beck, above the landing. A little narrow thing, black, low in the water, that you could hardly see even close. He got <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> into it, lying flat in the bottom on the wet boards, and got in himself in the stern, and took up a short paddle, and pushed off.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p47 -->
<p><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> lay on his back in the bottom of the boat and looked up at the stars and listened to Fisk paddle.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p48 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">It was slow going. Fisk went up the <sub alias="Salt-reach">Saltreach</sub> shore in the dark, in the reeds, away from the landing and the fires, a long way, until the noise of the men at the <sub alias="Eel-weer">Eelweir</sub> was faint behind them. Then out. Across. Into the main river, black and fast with the thaw, and the eel-boat swinging and Fisk grunting at the paddle. Then reeds again. Thick, tall, rattling dry over <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>’s face. Twisting. Left and right and left. Fisk paddling one stroke and stopping and listening and paddling another.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p49 -->
<p>Then the boat grounded, soft, on mud.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p50 · humour: dry gruff kindness · Fisk, breathed -->
<p><prosody volume="-5dB">“North shore,”</prosody> breathed Fisk. <prosody volume="-5dB">“Under the oaks. You’re on it.”</prosody> He didn’t get out. <prosody volume="-5dB">“I’ll be in the reeds. Here. If you need taking off. Three nights. After three nights I’ll think you’re dead and go home and get drunk.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p51 · Calder -->
<p>“Fisk…”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p52 · Fisk -->
<p>“Go on,” said Fisk. “Before I think better of it.”</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p53 -->
<p>He went up through the oaks in the dark.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p54 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">He’d been on the island once before. In the freeze, in the snow, with the dragons a league and a half off and their voices in his head. It was different now. No snow. Wet black earth and last year’s leaves, and the oaks dripping, and the smell of the river and mud and green things starting. And no voices. Only the feeling, very far off, the three of them on their far hill.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p55 -->
<p>He found the pit.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p56 -->
<p>He didn’t know it was there. He nearly walked into it. He came through the oaks at the north end of the island in the dark and the ground in front of him was suddenly gone, and he stopped with his toes at the edge of it and looked down.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p57 -->
<p>A great hole. Black. He couldn’t see the bottom. Raw banks of earth piled round it higher than his head, with the smell of wet clay. And at the bottom, when his eyes found it, a gleam of water. And under the water, something pale. Stone.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p58 -->
<p>He crouched at the edge and put his hand down on the bank.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p59 -->
<p>It was warm.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p60 · quiet: hushed discovery -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">He felt along it. A long hollow, pressed down into the clay at the edge of the pit, the length of a cart. The shape of something that had lain there a long time. The clay at the bottom of it was warm under his hand, warm as a bed somebody’s just got out of.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p61 -->
<p>And beside it, against the side of it, a smaller hollow. A person’s. Where somebody had sat with their back against something large. He put his hand in it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p62 -->
<p>Warm too. Less. But warm.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p63 -->
<p>He crouched in the dark at the edge of the pit with one hand in each.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p64 · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Hours,</prosody> he thought. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Not more.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p65 -->
<p>He found the rest at the west shore.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p66 -->
<p>He went down along the stream in the first grey, the way he’d gone in the snow, to the place where her fire had been. No fire now. But down at the water, where the stream came out into the reeds and the west channel, the mud was soft from the thaw, and it had kept everything.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p67 -->
<p>A keel-mark. A skiff’s. Pulled up into the reeds and pushed off again; the long groove of it in the mud, with the water just beginning to seep into the bottom of it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p68 -->
<p>Boot-prints. Narrow. A woman’s. He knew them. He’d knelt by them in the snow. Going down to the skiff.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p69 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">Beside them, going into the water, the others. Round. Each wider than both his hands spread. Four toes splayed and the claws drawn in. The edges of them still sharp in the mud. Still wet. Going down the bank and into the channel and not coming back.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p70 -->
<p>He stood in the reeds in the grey light and looked across the west channel at the <sub alias="Harthmeer">Hearthmere</sub> bank. At the long low fields going up from the water, wet and brown. At the tower at Redwatch on its rise across the water, with a light at the top still burning. At a dark line on the edge of the sky to the west that was a forest.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p71 -->
<p>Nothing moving. Nobody.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p72 · reflective · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="96%"><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">She took it off the island,</prosody> he thought. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Before they came. In the dark. She knew they were coming and she took it away.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p73 · Liss (remembered) -->
<p>He thought of Liss Ashdown’s face on the ferry, looking up at the cloud where <sub alias="Liska">Lisska</sub> had gone. <prosody volume="-3dB">Cast out yesterday. For loving a monster.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p74 · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">And it went with her.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p75 -->
<p>He went to the arch.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p76 -->
<p>The light was coming. Grey, then paler. The fort came up out of the dark round him, the broken walls and the ivy and the two gates, the way he’d seen it in the snow. The tower at the north-west corner, squat and round, broken off at the top. The arch in its side, with the dark doorway, and the lion’s long smooth hollow worn into the threshold.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p77 -->
<p>Over the arch, the verse.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p78 · awe -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">He couldn’t read it. He’d known he couldn’t. Six lines of square spare letters with little hooks at the ends of the strokes, cut deep and worn shallow, with the lichen grown into the cuts. He stood under it in the grey light and looked at it and it meant nothing at all.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p79 -->
<p>He took out the paper.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p80 -->
<p>He had to stand on a fallen block to reach. He held the sheet flat against the stone over the first line with one hand, the six hands showing on the side away from him, and took the charcoal in the other, and rubbed. Flat, hard, the way his father had done it at the boundary stone. And the letters came up white out of the black, line by line, where the cuts were, and where the stone was gone the black went smooth and empty.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p81 -->
<p>He did it twice, to be sure. When he’d done, he had two sheets, black, with six lines of white hooked letters on each, and gaps.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p82 -->
<p>He rolled them up and put them inside his shirt against his chest. He’d carry them to <sub alias="Orveth">Aurveth</sub>. He’d carry them to the hatching caves if he had to.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p83 -->
<p>He went into the tower.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p84 -->
<p>He hadn’t meant to. In the snow he hadn’t. He’d stood under the arch and felt <sub alias="Orveth">Aurveth</sub> stop reading, a league and a half off, and something had told him not to. He went in now because the light was coming and he needed to be out of it, and because he wanted to know.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p85 -->
<p>It was dark inside. A round room, the ground floor of the tower, with walls a man’s arm-span thick and the floor deep in old leaves and fallen stone and the droppings of owls. A stair went up round the inside of the wall, broken, to a floor above that was half gone, with the grey sky showing through the top of the tower beyond it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p86 -->
<p>And a stair went down.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p87 -->
<p>In the middle of the floor. A square hole, half choked with fallen stone and old black earth that had washed in over the years, and steps going down into it. Stone steps, worn hollow in the middle. He could see four of them, and then the fallen stone.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p88 -->
<p>He knelt at the edge of it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p89 -->
<p>Below the fourth step, where the fallen stone had slid away to one side, there was something. Grey. Flat. A slab of dressed stone, set in the floor of whatever was down there, flush. He could just see the edge of it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p90 -->
<p>He reached down. He moved one of the fallen stones, carefully, and then another.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p91 -->
<p>In the slab, in the middle of it, there was an iron plate. Black. Old. As big as a man’s hand spread out. And in the middle of the plate, a hole.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p92 -->
<p>A keyhole.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p93 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">He knelt there looking at it.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p94 · dread · Aurveth (remembered) -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">He didn’t know what it was. He’d never seen anything like it. A lock, in a slab of stone, in the floor at the bottom of a choked stair, under a tower on an island nobody landed on. He thought of the pit at the north end, and the pale stone at the bottom of it under the water. He thought of <sub alias="Orveth">Aurveth</sub> on the ice at midwinter with her throat to it. <prosody volume="-3dB">Something vast. Bound. Turning.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p95 -->
<p>He thought of his mother at <sub alias="Bell">Bel</sub>’s table with her hands flat on the wood.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p96 · Ada (remembered) -->
<p><prosody volume="-3dB">When two hands open what one hand shut.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p97 -->
<p>He put the fallen stones back, carefully, the way they’d been.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p98 -->
<p>The boats came at full light.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p99 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">He heard them before he saw them. From the east. From across the east channel, from the <sub alias="Eel-weer">Eelweir</sub>. A sound like a great many people talking at once a long way off, and the knock of oars, and men shouting the stroke. Closer. Then the scrape of keels on the gravel of the spit at the south end, one after another after another, dozens of them, and boots on stones, and voices, a lot of voices, coming up from the landing toward the fort.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p100 -->
<p>He went up the stair.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p101 -->
<p>The broken stair round the inside of the tower wall. Up, in the half-dark, on steps that rocked under him, to the floor above that was half gone. Joists, and a few rotten boards, and a great beam that had come down from the roof long ago and lay slantwise across the corner where the floor still held, with a gap under it. He crawled under the beam and lay flat on the old boards in the dust and the owl-droppings, with his face to a crack in the wall where a stone had come out, and looked down.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p102 -->
<p>He could see the arch from there. And the fort. And through the broken top of the eastern gate, a long slice of the slope going down to the spit.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p103 -->
<p>They came up it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p104 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">Men. Hundreds of them. In grey, most of them, with the six on their coats, and others in the coats of the companies, the red hand, the black ship, Delling’s yellow tower. With spears and crossbows. With carts, little ones, hauled by hand up from the boats. With cages on the carts. Iron cages, big ones, empty. With long ladders. With beasts.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p105 -->
<p>The hounds came first. In packs. Silent. With their handlers walking behind them with an arm up.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p106 -->
<p>They came in through the eastern gate of the fort and spread out across it, and the noise filled the old walls like water filling a cistern.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p107 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">He lay under the beam and watched them take the island.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p108 -->
<p>They were everywhere by mid-morning.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p109 -->
<p>In the fort. In the oaks. Along the shores. A company went down the stream to the west shore, and he saw them stop at the reeds where the skiff had been and stand there, looking at the ground. Pointing. One of them knelt down. Then they all came back up the stream fast, and a man went running toward the arch, toward a knot of men standing by the western gate.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p110 · Calder (thought) -->
<p>A company went north with chains. He saw them go, through the oaks toward the pit. Men with the long coiled beast-chains over their shoulders and the six on every link, and men with clay pots on poles, smoking, a thick sweet smoke that came back on the wind and made his eyes water even up in the tower. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Honey-smoke.</prosody> Hounds in front. Going carefully, spread out.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p111 -->
<p>They came back an hour later. The chains still coiled on their shoulders. The pots still smoking. Nothing between them.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p112 -->
<p>Somebody below the tower was shouting.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p113 -->
<p>He pressed his face to the crack.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p114 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">By the arch, under the verse, a man stood with his back to the tower. Tall. Thin. Dark hair cut short. Very still. The man who’d run from the west shore was standing in front of him, talking fast, pointing west, across the channel. The tall man didn’t look where he was pointing. He stood under the verse with his hands behind his back and listened, and when the man had finished he said something, one word, <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> couldn’t hear it, and the man went away again fast.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p115 -->
<p>The tall man stood under the arch a while longer.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p116 -->
<p>He turned his head, and looked up. At the verse. At the six lines of old letters over the doorway, worn and lichened. He looked at them a long while. Reading them, <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> thought. He could read them.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p117 -->
<p>He wasn’t the only one. A little behind him stood a small grey man in a long brown gown gone ragged at the hem, like a chapel-man’s, with his head tipped back and his lips moving. The men round the arch left a space round him, as men leave a space round a dog they don’t know.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p118 -->
<p>He looked down. At the threshold of the arch. At the long smooth hollow worn in the stone, where something huge had lain longer than anyone could say.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p119 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">He crouched down and put his hand in it.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p120 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%"><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> lay under the beam with his face to the crack in the wall and didn’t breathe.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p121 -->
<p>It was the face from the back room at the Ram’s Head. The one who’d sat at the table writing while Rook stood beside him. It was the face from the <sub alias="Tide-steps">Tidesteps</sub>, too, he thought. It must be. Vale. But it crouched under the arch with its hand flat in the lion’s hollow as still as the Ram’s Head face had been, and he couldn’t make it be the same as the laughing one on the <sub alias="Tide-steps">Tidesteps</sub>, throwing his arms out.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p122 -->
<p>The tall man stood up.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p123 -->
<p>He said something to the men behind him, quietly. Two of them came forward with crowbars and went into the tower under <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>, into the dark room below, and he heard them start to shift the fallen stone from the stair.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p124 -->
<p>He lay under the beam all day.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p125 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">He didn’t move. He couldn’t. The men in the room below dug at the stair all morning, carrying the fallen stone out in baskets, and he lay above them on the rotten boards and listened to every basket go out. By noon they’d stopped. He heard the tall man’s voice below, quiet. Then nothing. Somebody laid something down on the floor, heavy, with a clink. Then two men sat down by the door of the tower and stayed there.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p126 -->
<p>Guards. On the stair. On the slab with the lock in it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p127 -->
<p>He lay above them.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p128 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">The island filled up all afternoon. More boats. More men. Fires lit in the fort. By the western gate a man sat on a crate with a pot of white paint and a stack of company shields beside him, painting marks on the face of each, small and square with hooks at the ends, quick, the way Ada wrote a tally. He never once looked up at the verse to copy them. He knew them already. Tents going up in the oaks. A cage brought up from the spit with something in it, something large and grey and silent, and set down by the eastern gate. The noise of a camp. Men laughing. Somebody singing one of the old songs, badly. <prosody rate="90%">Before the six, there was one.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p129 · tender -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">He lay under the beam with the rubbing against his chest under his shirt and the chains in his coat and Joss’s knife in his belt, and felt, very far away in the north, the three of them on their hill. Not happy. There.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p130 · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Not yet,</prosody> he thought at them, though they couldn’t hear words. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Not yet. Stay.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p131 -->
<p>He thought of a skiff’s keel-mark in the mud at the west shore, with the water just seeping into it. A woman’s narrow boot-prints. The lion’s prints going down into the channel beside them, the edges still sharp.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p132 · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Hours,</prosody> he thought. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">She missed them by hours.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p133 · grief · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%"><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">So did I.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p134 -->
<p>Below him, by the arch, somebody laughed, and somebody else shushed them, and the tall man’s quiet voice said something, and the island went on filling with men as the light went.</p>
<break time="1800ms"/>
</speak>
